# Definitions of a strange loop

The **definitions of a strange loop** are seven candidate definitions, ordered
from Hofstadter's original wording to a deliberately philosophical one. Only
the first is established usage. The other six were proposed in the discussion
this vault is built on, as refinements that differ in what they include in the
system and what they leave out. None of them is a standard technical term.

## Description

The request behind the list was to close the discussion with a series of
definitions: start from Hofstadter's, add four to seven options "depending on
what we have included or excluded from that system", and let the highlight
resemble the Daoist definition of the Dao, the incompleteness of a consistent
system, and a generalization of that.

### The seven

| | Name | Definition | Adds | Reference case |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Hofstadter | Moving up or down through the levels of an apparent hierarchy unexpectedly returns to the starting level | The original | Gödel, Escher, Bach |
| 2 | Structural | The representation of a structure takes part in a closed relation that returns to that structure | Drops the hierarchy; keeps representation and closure | Escher's hands |
| 3 | Reflective | A system can internally encode and interpret aspects of its own structure or operation | An encoding and an interpreter inside the system | A reflective formal system; a metacircular interpreter |
| 4 | Gödelian | An effective, sufficiently strong formal theory supports diagonal self-reference, yielding fixed-point statements with formal consequences such as incompleteness | A theorem about the consequences | The Gödel sentence |
| 5 | Generative | A system internally describes or reconstructs the principles of its own generation | Reconstruction in place of a sentence | The constructible universe |
| 6 | Evolving reflective | A system uses a model of itself to revise its future models, rules, or criteria for revision | Time and change | A progression of theories; a reflective agent |
| 7 | Epistemically bounded | A system represents aspects of itself but is subject to intrinsic limits on complete, internally justified self-description | A limit claimed for every such system | A conjecture |

Each has a card where its mechanism is worked out: [[level-crossing]] for the
first two, [[reflective-formal-system]] for the third and fourth,
[[constructible-universe]] for the fifth, [[reflection-progression]] for the
sixth.

### The core equations

Three of the definitions can be written down exactly.

The Gödelian one is a diagonal fixed point:

$$T\vdash G\leftrightarrow\neg\operatorname{Prov}_T(\ulcorner G\urcorner).$$

The generative one is internal reconstruction:

$$L_{\alpha+1}=\operatorname{Def}(L_\alpha),\qquad L^L=L.$$

The evolving reflective one is a step that changes both the theory and the
rule for changing it:

$$(T_n,R_n)\longrightarrow(T_{n+1},R_{n+1}).$$

### What the ordering shows

The seven are not rival answers to one question. Reading down the table, each
keeps self-representation and changes one other thing.

- From one to two, the hierarchy is dropped. What remains is a representation
  that closes on what it represents.
- From two to three, the representation moves inside the system, which now
  needs a way to encode itself and a way to read the encoding.
- From three to four, the system is required to prove something as a result.
  This is the difference between the existence of self-reference and a theorem
  about its consequences.
- Four and five are the two rigorous cases, and they work by different
  mechanisms. Four is diagonalization. Five is definability and absoluteness
  in a well-founded hierarchy, with no diagonal sentence and no feedback.
- Neither four nor five involves time. Six adds it, in answer to the proposal
  that a human who extends the axioms is what makes the loop essential. The
  correction recorded with it is that a human is not required: a program can
  run a progression of theories.
- Seven turns the result of four into a claim about every reflective system.

### Why the seventh is philosophy and not a theorem

Gödel and Tarski establish exact limitations under explicit hypotheses: an
effectively axiomatized, consistent, sufficiently strong theory is incomplete,
and arithmetical truth is not definable in arithmetic. The statement that
*every* sufficiently reflective intelligence faces an analogous limit is an
interesting conjecture. It does not follow from either theorem.

The Daoist line, that the Tao which can be spoken is not the enduring Tao,
says something of the same shape about descriptions and what they describe. It
is a philosophical analogy, and it proves nothing either. The seventh
definition is where the vault keeps that intuition, labelled as what it is.

### The definition preferred for work

The single definition the discussion settles on combines three, five and
seven, and guards the last with one word:

> A strange loop is a reflective structure in which a system becomes an object
> of its own representation, allowing it to describe, interpret or reconstruct
> aspects of itself, potentially exposing intrinsic limitations of its
> descriptive or reasoning framework.

*Potentially* prevents the false claim that every self-referential cycle is
incomplete or inconsistent. It is quoted and used on the [[strange-loop]]
card.

## Relations

- It refines the concept of the [[strange-loop]] {relation: generalizes}, whose card keeps the original sense.
- The first two definitions rest on [[level-crossing]] {relation: uses}.
- The third is the definition of a [[reflective-formal-system]] {relation: uses}.
- The fourth is realised by the [[godel-sentence]] {relation: example-of} and proved by [[diagonalization]] {relation: uses}.
- The fourth's formal consequence is stated in the [[incompleteness-theorems]] {relation: uses}.
- The fifth is realised by the [[constructible-universe]] {relation: example-of}.
- The sixth is realised by a [[reflection-progression]] {relation: example-of} and embodied in a [[reflective-agent]] {relation: example-of}.
- The seventh leans on [[tarski-undefinability]] {relation: analogous-to} and on the [[daoist-analogy]] {relation: analogous-to}, and concerns [[epistemic-limits]] {relation: uses}.
- It complements the [[taxonomy-of-self-reference]] {relation: contrasts}: the taxonomy sorts examples, these definitions state conditions.
- It is the vault's direct answer to the question [[strange-loop-meaning]] {relation: answers}.
- The sixth is the one an executable-document experiment has to meet, which bears on [[building-a-loop-in-hmd]] {relation: answers}.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[strange-loop]].** Hofstadter's notion of a return to the starting level
  through a crossing of levels. It is the first row of the table and the thing
  the other six try to sharpen.
- **[[godel-sentence]].** A sentence provably equivalent to the statement of
  its own unprovability. It is the reference case of the fourth definition
  and the only one with a theorem attached.
- **[[constructible-universe]].** Gödel's hierarchy of definable sets, which
  reconstructs itself when built inside itself. It is the reference case of
  the fifth definition.
- **[[reflection-progression]].** A sequence of theories, each extending the
  last, in which the criteria for extension may themselves be revised. It is
  the reference case of the sixth.
- **[[taxonomy-of-self-reference]].** Eight classes of self-referential
  systems, sorted by mechanism. It was the other response to the lack of a
  formal definition.

